/' + $('#other_games_select').val();})"]] " | " [:a {:href "/event_names/380"} "Event type"]] [:h1 {:class "event_title"} "Coxless Pairs, Men" [:small {}]] [:table {:class "biodata"} [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} "17 – 23 September 2000"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/753"} "Sydney International Regatta Centre, Penrith, New South Wales"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/159589"} "30 from 15 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "With the two-time defending Olympic champions from Great Britain, " [:a {:href "/athletes/37440"} "Steven Redgrave"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/37428"} "Matthew Pinsent"] ", having switched disciplines, the men’s coxless pairs event at the 2000 Games was much more open than it had been at the past. Australia’s " [:a {:href ""} "James Tomkins"] ", a double Olympic champion in the coxless fours from 1992 and 1996, was one half of the reigning World Champions, but his partner, " [:a {:href "/athletes/35421"} "Drew Ginn"] ", suffered a back injury and could not attend his home Olympics, and thus Tomkins brought " [:a {:href "/athletes/89319"} "Matthew Long"] " instead. The other two recent World Champions, France and Germany, sent their victorious crews to the Games. France, represented by " [:a {:href "/athletes/36844"} "Michel Andrieux"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/37083"} "Jean-Christophe Rolland"] ", was the 1997 World champion, the 1999 World runner-up, and the 1996 Olympic bronze medalist. Germany, meanwhile, had " [:a {:href "/athletes/92589"} "Robert Sens"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/37880"} "Detlef Kirchhoff"] ", the 1998 World Champions. Kirchhoff also had three previous Olympic medals in other disciplines. Great Britain would attempt to defend its title with " [:a {:href "/athletes/92555"} "Ed Coode"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/37460"} "Greg Searle"] ", a 1992 Olympic champion in the coxed pairs and 1996 bronze medalist in the coxless fours."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The Britons posted the fastest time in the opening round, with Serbia and Montenegro (the Federal Republic of Yugoslavia) only 0.17 seconds slower in winning their own heat with " [:a {:href "/athletes/92747"} "Đorđe Višacki"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/92746"} "Nikola Stojić"] ". France was the slowest of the three in capturing its own heat, but was fastest in the semi-finals, with Australia barely emerging victorious in its own heat against Serbia and Montenegro. In the final, France retained its strength and won gold by a margin of a nearly a second, while the American duo of " [:a {:href "/athletes/40811"} "Ted Murphy"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/92712"} "Sebastian Bea"] " snuck in for a surprise second-place finish. Murphy had been one half of the bronze medal-winning boat at the 1997 World Championships and had earned a silver medal in the coxed fours in 1994, while Bea was a 1997 gold medalist in the coxed eights. The race for the final podium spot was a tight one, with Australia and Great Britain battling to the line, where the former nabbed the bronze with only 0.14 seconds to spare."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Pair"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/36844"} "Michel Andrieux"] " / " [:a {:href "/athletes/37083"} "Jean-Christophe Rolland"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/FRA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/FRA.png"}] "FRA"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/40811"} "Ted Murphy"] " / " [:a {:href "/athletes/92712"} "Sebastian Bea"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/USA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flag